<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis of Assisi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1181–<a href="/years/1226">1226</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni di Pietro di Bernardone, Saint Francis of Assisi, Francesco d'Assisi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, born Giovanni di Pietro di Bernardone, was an Italian mystic, poet and Catholic friar who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>. The son of a wealthy family of Assisi, he turned as a young man to poverty and devotion, repairing chapels and caring for the sick, and after a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> he received approval for the Order of Friars Minor. In 1219 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in an attempt to convert the sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">Al-Kamil</a> and end the conflict of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a>, and at Greccio he arranged the first live <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-scene">nativity scene</a>. According to Christian tradition he received the stigmata in 1224; he died in 1226 and was canonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> in 1228.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi.jpg" alt="Francis of Assisi" width="330" height="600" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of San Francesco Bardi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Master_of_San_Francesco_Bardi,_St._Francis_and_scenes_from_his_life.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
